---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Figure from '../components/Figure.astro';
import ContextLine from '../components/ContextLine.astro';
import EventStudy from '../figures/EventStudy/EventStudy';
import { FIG1_PARAMS, FIG1_TAG } from '../figures/EventStudy/meta';
import { u } from '../lib/url';
---
<BaseLayout title="Event study lab" description="The full version of Fig. 1: an interactive event study on simulated returns, with adjustable estimation and event windows and three expected-return models.">
  <div class="wrap lab">
    <header class="prose head">
      <h1>Event study lab</h1>
      <ContextLine items={['Interactive figure', 'simulated returns', 'market model, market-adjusted, mean-adjusted']} />
    </header>
    <p class="prose">
      This is the full version of Fig. 1 on the home page. The returns are simulated with a fixed seed, so every visitor
      sees the same series. The Cobre Panamá paper runs the same code on real prices.
    </p>

    <Figure n={1} title="Event study of a one-day shock" tag={FIG1_TAG} params={FIG1_PARAMS}>
      <EventStudy client:load id="lab" source={{ kind: 'sim' }} size="full" />
      <Fragment slot="caption">
        <p>CAR summed from day −10, with a 95% band of ±1.96·<span class="hat">σ</span>·√k after k days. The table refers to the event window only.</p>
      </Fragment>
    </Figure>

    <section class="section prose" aria-labelledby="how">
      <h2 id="how">How the numbers are computed</h2>
      <ul class="steps">
        <li><span class="hat">α</span> and <span class="hat tall">β</span> come from an OLS regression of the firm's daily return on the market return over the estimation window.</li>
        <li>The abnormal return is AR<sub>t</sub> = R<sub>t</sub> − (<span class="hat">α</span> + <span class="hat tall">β</span>·R<sub>m,t</sub>). CAR is the sum of AR over the event window.</li>
        <li>
          SE(CAR) = <span class="hat">σ</span>(ε)·√L, where L is the number of days in the event window. This ignores the estimation error in <span class="hat">α</span> and <span class="hat tall">β</span> and any autocorrelation in the residuals, so with a short estimation window the SE comes out slightly too small.
        </li>
        <li>t = CAR / SE(CAR). The p-value is two-sided, from a t distribution with N − 2 degrees of freedom (N − 1 for the two models with fixed coefficients).</li>
        <li>Market-adjusted fixes α = 0 and β = 1. Mean-adjusted uses the firm's average return over the estimation window as the expected return.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="section prose" aria-labelledby="controls">
      <h2 id="controls">Controls</h2>
      <p>
        Drag the ends of either bar. With a keyboard, tab to a handle and use the arrow keys to move it one day, or Page Up
        and Page Down to move it 5 days on the event window and 10 on the estimation window. The estimation window is at
        least 30 days long and ends by day −11.
      </p>
      <p class="more"><a href={u('/')}>← Home</a></p>
    </section>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
<style>
  .head { padding: var(--space-5) 0 var(--space-3); }
  .head h1 { margin-bottom: var(--space-2); }
  .steps { padding-left: 1.2rem; }
  .steps li { margin-bottom: 0.6rem; }
  /* the full version breaks out of the 900px column, centred, up to 1200px */
  .lab :global(.fig) {
    --full: min(1200px, calc(100vw - 2 * var(--gutter)));
    width: var(--full);
    max-width: none;
    margin-left: calc((100% - var(--full)) / 2);
  }
</style>
